<section xml:id="sec-7-1-Angles" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title component="not-workbook">Angles</title>
	<title component="workbook">Angles - Practice exercises</title>

	<subsection xml:id="ss-7-1-neighborhood-angles" component="not-workbook">
        <title>Neighborhood angles</title>
        <p>
            Tim is walking around his neighborhood in St. Paul, obsessing about angles. He sees angles, like, everywhere.
        </p>

        <p>
            Tim notices the stately oak, the energetic maple, and the perfectly formed linden tree all have branches sticking out at funny angles <ndash/> perhaps 45<degree /> for the linden, 60<degree /> for the maple, and up to 90<degree /> for the oak.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig_branch_angles">
            <image width="50%" source="tree_branches.png"/>
            <caption>Branch angles. Source: Gemini</caption>
        </figure>

        <p>
            Tim compares the roof lines of the 1920s one-and-a-half story craftsman bungalows with their low-slung roofs at about 20<degree /> to the 1940s two-story tudors with their dramatic 40<degree /> pitched roofs.
        </p>

        <figure xml:id="fig_roof_lines">
            <image width="60%" source="bungalow_tudor.png"/>
            <caption>Comparing roof angles on a bungalow (left) and tudor (right). Source: Gemini</caption>
        </figure>

        <p>
            He passes the local skate park and sees a few teens practicing their spins. A quick 180<degree /> frontside (where you end up facing the opposite direction), a 360<degree /> aerial (where you end up facing the same direction you started), and one kid even pulled off a 540<degree /> aerial!
        </p>

        <!-- <figure xml:id="fig_540_aerial">
            <image width="50%" source="skateboard.png"/>
            <caption>A 540<degree/> aerial on a skateboard. Source: Gemini</caption>
        </figure> -->
	</subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-7-1-angles-in-triangles" component="not-workbook">
        <title>Angles in triangles</title>

        <p>
            Tim learned in geometry class that angles can be measured between any two lines. For example each pair of sides in a triangle has an angle between them. A fun fact is that the three angles in a triangle add up to 180<degree />, the same number of degrees as a straight line.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, the triangular roof on the bungalow house has two angles of 20<degree />, which means the larger angle is
            <md>
                180^{\circ}-20^{\circ}-20^{\circ}=140^{\circ}
            </md>
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-bungalow-roof">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Coordinates
                \coordinate (A) at (0, 0);
                \coordinate (B) at (6, 0);
                % Height h = 3 * tan(20°) ≈ 1.092
                \coordinate (C) at (3, 1.092);

                % Draw Roof Triangle (unfilled / white background)
                \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

                % Bottom-left angle arc and lowered label (20°)
                \draw (0.8,0) arc (0:20:0.8);
                \node at (1.3, 0.2) {$20^\circ$};

                % Bottom-right angle arc and lowered label (20°)
                \draw (5.2,0) arc (180:160:0.8);
                \node at (4.7, 0.2) {$20^\circ$};

                % Top angle arc tighter to vertex (C), label lowered beneath the arc
                \draw (2.7, 0.983) arc (200:340:0.32);
                \node at (3, 0.6) {$140^\circ$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            There's a name for a triangle where two angles are equal. It's called an <alert>isoceles</alert> triangle. The roof on the bungalow house (and the tudor house) form isoceles triangles. A fun fact about isoceles triangles is that the sides across from the matching angles have the same length. For example, the roof might have these dimensions.
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-bungalow-roof-sides">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Coordinates
                \coordinate (A) at (0, 0);
                \coordinate (B) at (6, 0);
                % Height h = 3 * tan(20°) ≈ 1.092
                \coordinate (C) at (3, 1.092);

                % Draw Roof Triangle (unfilled / white background)
                \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

                % Bottom-left angle arc and lowered label (20°)
                \draw (0.8,0) arc (0:20:0.8);
                \node at (1.3, 0.2) {$20^\circ$};

                % Bottom-right angle arc and lowered label (20°)
                \draw (5.2,0) arc (180:160:0.8);
                \node at (4.7, 0.2) {$20^\circ$};

                % Top angle arc tighter to vertex (C), label lowered beneath the arc
                \draw (2.7, 0.983) arc (200:340:0.32);
                \node at (3, 0.6) {$140^\circ$};

                % Side Labels
                \node[below] at (3, 0) {26 feet};
                \node[above left, sloped] at (1.5, 0.546) {13.83 feet};
                \node[above right, sloped] at (4.5, 0.546) {13.83 feet};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-7-1-special-triangle" component="not-workbook">
        <title>A special triangle</title>
        <p>
            The 60<degree /> of the maple tree branch sits within a right triangle. The missing angle is
            <md>
                180^{\circ}-90^{\circ}-60^{\circ} = 30^{\circ}.
            </md>
            Notice if we measure <m>a=1</m> foot up the tree and <m>b</m> feet out from the tree, it's not immediately clear what <m>b</m> equals. We can label the length of the corresponding branch <m>c</m> feet.
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-special-triangle">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Coordinates
                \coordinate (A) at (0, 3);          % Right-angle corner (top-left)
                \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);          % Bottom corner
                \coordinate (C) at (1.732*3, 3);    % Top-right corner (b = sqrt(3) ≈ 1.732 relative to a = 1)

                % Draw Triangle (a=1 vertical leg, b=sqrt(3) horizontal top leg, c=2 hypotenuse)
                \draw[thick] (A) -- (C) -- (B) node[midway, sloped, below] {$c \text{ feet}$} -- cycle;

                % Right angle symbol
                \draw (0, 2.8) -- (0.2, 2.8) -- (0.2, 3);

                % Bottom-left 60° angle arc and label
                \draw (0, 0.5) arc (90:30:0.5);
                \node at (0.4, 0.75) {$60^\circ$};

                % Top-right 30° angle arc and label
                \draw (1.732*3 - 0.5, 3) arc (180:210:0.5);
                \node at (1.732*3 - 1, 2.75) {$30^\circ$};

                % Side Labels
                \node[left, rotate=90, anchor=south] at (0, 1.5) {$a = 1\text{ foot}$};
                \node[above] at (1.732*1.25, 3) {$b\text{ feet}$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            Let's figure out what <m>b</m> and <m>c</m> are. Look what happens if we put an upside-down copy of the triangle on top of our triangle.
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-top-bottom">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Coordinates for Bottom Triangle
                \coordinate (A) at (0, 3);          % Shared Right-angle corner
                \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);          % Bottom-left corner
                \coordinate (C) at (1.732*3, 3);    % Far-right corner

                % Coordinates for Top Triangle
                \coordinate (B2) at (0, 6);         % Top-left corner

                % --- BOTTOM TRIANGLE ---
                % Base (b) normal thickness, sides (a and c) made very thick
                \draw (A) -- (C);
                \draw[very thick] (C) -- (B) node[midway, sloped, below] {$c\text{ feet}$};
                \draw[very thick] (A) -- (B);

                % Bottom triangle right angle symbol
                \draw (0, 2.8) -- (0.2, 2.8) -- (0.2, 3);

                % Bottom-left 60° angle arc and label
                \draw (0, 0.5) arc (90:30:0.5);
                \node at (0.4, 0.75) {$60^\circ$};

                % Bottom top-right 30° angle arc and label
                \draw (1.732*3 - 0.5, 3) arc (180:210:0.5);
                \node at (1.732*3 - 1, 2.75) {$30^\circ$};

                % Bottom side label (rotated sideways)
                \node[left, rotate=90, anchor=south] at (0, 1.5) {$a = 1\text{ foot}$};

                % --- TOP TRIANGLE ---
                \draw[very thick] (C) -- (B2) node[midway, sloped, above] {$c \text{ feet}$};
                \draw[very thick] (A) -- (B2);

                % Top triangle right angle symbol
                \draw (0, 3.2) -- (0.2, 3.2) -- (0.2, 3);

                % Top-left 60° angle arc and label
                \draw (0, 5.5) arc (270:330:0.5);
                \node at (0.4, 5.25) {$60^\circ$};

                % Top top-right 30° angle arc and label
                \draw (1.732*3 - 0.5, 3) arc (180:150:0.5);
                \node at (1.732*3 - 1, 3.25) {$30^\circ$};

                % Top side label (rotated sideways)
                \node[left, rotate=90, anchor=south] at (0, 4.5) {$a = 1\text{ foot}$};

                % --- SHARED MIDDLE SIDE LABEL (b) ---
                \node[above] at (1.732*1.25, 3) {$b\text{ feet}$};

                % --- FULL LENGTH DIMENSION BAR (2 feet) ---
                % Dimension line shifted left to x = -1.2
                \draw[&lt;-, &gt;=latex] (-1.2, 0) -- (-1.2, 2.7);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex] (-1.2, 3.3) -- (-1.2, 6);
                % End caps (ticks) at top and bottom
                \draw (-1.35, 0) -- (-1.05, 0);
                \draw (-1.35, 6) -- (-1.05, 6);
                % Text label centered along the dimension bar
                \node[left] at (-.75, 3) {$2\text{ feet}$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            Notice that the sides labeled <m>b</m> line up and the sides labeled <m>a</m> fall exactly in a straight line because
            <md>
                90^{\circ} + 90^{\circ} = 180^{\circ}
            </md>
            That entire side of the big outer triangle is length <m>1+1=2</m> feet.
        </p>

        <p>
            The angles of the outer triangle are 60<degree  />, 60<degree  />, and <m>30^{\circ} + 30^{\circ}=60^{\circ}</m>, which means all three angles are equal. By symmetry, the lengths of the sides must all be equal too. Since the left-hand side has length 2 feet, we get <m>c=2</m> feet also.
        </p>

        <p>
            By the way, when all three angles and all three sides of a triangle are equal, it is an <alert>equilateral triangle</alert>. So an isoceles triangles has two angles and two sides equal, but an equilateral triangle has three angles and three sides equal.
        </p>

        <p>
            Let's go back to our original triangle and use the Pythagorean Theorem to find the length <m>b</m>. We know
            <md>
                a^{2} + b^{2} = c^{2}.
            </md>
            Evaluating at <m>a=1</m> and <m>c=2</m> we get
            <md>
                1^{2} + b^{2} = 2^{2}
            </md>
            That is,
            <md>
                1 + b^{2} = 4
            </md>
            so subtracting 1 from each side we get <m>b^{2} = 3</m>. Therefore <m>b = \sqrt{3}\approx 1.732 \text{ feet}</m>.
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-sqrt-3">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Coordinates
                \coordinate (A) at (0, 3);          % Right-angle corner (top-left)
                \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);          % Bottom corner
                \coordinate (C) at (1.732*3, 3);    % Top-right corner (b = sqrt(3) ≈ 1.732 relative to a = 1)

                % Draw Triangle (a=1 vertical leg, b=sqrt(3) horizontal top leg, c=2 hypotenuse)
                \draw[thick] (A) -- (C) -- (B) node[midway, sloped, below] {$c = 2\text{ feet}$} -- cycle;

                % Right angle symbol
                \draw (0, 2.8) -- (0.2, 2.8) -- (0.2, 3);

                % Bottom-left 60° angle arc and label
                \draw (0, 0.5) arc (90:30:0.5);
                \node at (0.35, 0.75) {$60^\circ$};

                % Top-right 30° angle arc and label
                \draw (1.732*3 - 0.5, 3) arc (180:210:0.5);
                \node at (1.732*3 - 1, 2.75) {$30^\circ$};

                % Side Labels
                \node[left] at (0, 1.5) {$a = 1\text{ foot}$};
                \node[above] at (1.732*1.5, 3) {$b = \sqrt{3} \approx 1.732\text{ feet}$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            This is a <alert>special triangle</alert> because there were nice clean numbers for the lengths. In a precalculus or calculus class, you might see this triangle drawn in various positions.
        </p>

        <sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="auto" valign="middle">

            <!--% Triangle in branch position-->
            <image>
                <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-branch-pos">
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.75]
                    % Coordinates
                    \coordinate (A) at (0, 3);          % Right-angle corner (top-left)
                    \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);          % Bottom corner
                    \coordinate (C) at (1.732*3, 3);    % Top-right corner (b = sqrt(3) ≈ 1.732 relative to a = 1)

                    % Draw Triangle (a=1 vertical leg, b=sqrt(3) horizontal top leg, c=2 hypotenuse)
                    \draw[thick] (A) -- (C) -- (B) node[midway, sloped, below] {2} -- cycle;

                    % Right angle symbol
                    \draw (0, 2.8) -- (0.2, 2.8) -- (0.2, 3);

                    % Bottom-left 60° angle arc and label
                    \draw (0, 0.5) arc (90:30:0.5);
                    \node at (0.5, 0.75) {$60^\circ$};

                    % Top-right 30° angle arc and label
                    \draw (1.732*3 - 0.5, 3) arc (180:210:0.5);
                    \node at (1.732*3 - 1, 2.75) {$30^\circ$};

                    % Side Labels
                    \node[left] at (0, 1.5) {1};
                    \node[above] at (1.732*1.5, 3) {$ \sqrt{3}$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
            <!--%%%%%%%-->
            <!--% Triangle in standard 60 degree position-->
            <image>
                <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-std-60-pos">
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.75]
                    % Coordinates: 
                    % Side 1 (horizontal): length = 3
                    % Side \sqrt{3} (vertical to the right): length = 1.732 * 3 ≈ 5.196
                    \coordinate (A) at (3, 0);          % Right-angle corner (bottom-right)
                    \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);          % Bottom-left corner
                    \coordinate (C) at (3, 1.732*3);    % Top-right corner

                    % Draw Triangle
                    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) node[midway, sloped, above] {2} -- cycle;

                    % Right angle symbol (at bottom-right)
                    \draw (2.8, 0) -- (2.8, 0.2) -- (3, 0.2);

                    % Bottom-left 60° angle arc and label
                    \draw (0.5, 0) arc (0:60:0.5);
                    \node at (1, 0.5) {$60^\circ$};

                    % Top-right 30° angle arc and label
                    \draw (3, 1.732*3 - 0.5) arc (270:240:0.5);
                    \node at (2.65, 1.732*3 - 1.5) {$30^\circ$};

                    % Side Labels
                    \node[below] at (1.5, 0) {1};
                    \node[right] at (3, 1.732*1.5) {$\sqrt{3}$};
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>
            <!--%%%%%%%-->
            <!--% Triangle in standard 30 degree position-->
            <image>
                <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-std-30-pos">
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=.75]
                    % Coordinates:
                    % Side \sqrt{3} (horizontal bottom): length = 1.732 * 3 ≈ 5.196
                    % Side 1 (vertical to the right): length = 3
                    \coordinate (A) at (1.732*3, 0);    % Right-angle corner (bottom-right)
                    \coordinate (B) at (0, 0);          % Bottom-left corner
                    \coordinate (C) at (1.732*3, 3);    % Top-right corner

                    % Draw Triangle
                    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) node[midway, sloped, above] {2} -- cycle;

                    % Right angle symbol (at bottom-right)
                    \draw (1.732*3 - 0.2, 0) -- (1.732*3 - 0.2, 0.2) -- (1.732*3, 0.2);

                    % Bottom-left 30° angle arc and label
                    \draw (0.5, 0) arc (0:30:0.5);
                    \node at (1.3, 0.3) {$30^\circ$};

                    % Top-right 60° angle arc and label
                    \draw (1.732*3, 3 - 0.5) arc (270:210:0.5);
                    \node at (1.732*3 - 0.5, 2.2) {$60^\circ$};

                    % Side Labels
                    \node[below] at (1.732*1.5, 0) {$\sqrt{3}$};
                    \node[right] at (1.732*3, 1.5) {1};
                \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
            </image>

        </sidebyside>

        <p>
            In this <alert>30-60-90 triangle</alert>, the hypotenuse is always length 2, the shortest side (of length 1) is across from (or <q>opposite</q>) to the smaller 30<degree  /> angle, and the longer side opposite to the larger 60<degree  /> angle is length <m>\sqrt{3}</m>.
        </p>

        <p>
            You will see another special triangle, the <alert>isoceles right triangle</alert> (or <alert>45-45-90 triangle</alert>, in the exercises.
        </p>
        
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="ss-7-1-std-pos" component="not-workbook">
        <title>Angles in standard position</title>
        <p>
            Not all angles fit in a right triangle or even any shape triangle. Like that 540<degree /> rotation of the skateboarder. A general way to measure angles is to start the measure on the positive horizontal axis, let's label it the <m>x</m>-axis and rotate counterclockwise around. In case you're not familiar with the hands on an analog clock, that means we head towards the positive vertical axis, which we'll label the <m>y</m>-axis. For example, the picture shows measuring angles of 30<degree  />, 45<degree  />, 60<degree  />, 90<degree  />, and 180<degree  />.
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-angles-1">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw Axes (without tick marks)
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3.7, 0) -- (3.7, 0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0, -0.5) -- (0, 3.7) node[above] {$y$};

                % --- 30° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (30:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.0, 0) arc (0:30:1.0);
                \node[above right] at (5:0.9) {$30^\circ$};

                % --- 45° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (45:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.5, 0) arc (0:45:1.5);
                \node[above right] at (37:1.4) {$45^\circ$};

                % --- 60° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (60:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.0, 0) arc (0:60:2);
                \node[above right] at (54:1.9) {$60^\circ$};

                % --- 90° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on positive y-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.5, 0) arc (0:90:2.5);
                \node[above right] at (88:2.5) {$90^\circ$};

                % --- 180° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on negative x-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (3.0, 0) arc (0:180:3.0);
                \node[above left] at (175:3.0) {$180^\circ$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            Note that the straight angle, 180<degree />, is halfway around the circle. A full rotation is 360<degree />. That 540<degree />? It's one and half times around because
            <md>
                360^{\circ} + 180^{\circ} = 540^{\circ}
            </md>
            In our picture we also include three quarters of the way around which is <m>3 \times 90^{\circ} = 270^{\circ}</m> Note that 0<degree /> is just where we start.
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-angles-2">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw Axes (without tick marks)
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3.7, 0) -- (3.7, 0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0, -3.7) -- (0, 3.7) node[above] {$y$};

                % --- 0° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on positive x-axis (no arc)
                \node[above right] at (0.8, 0) {$0^\circ$};

                % --- 90° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on positive y-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.3, 0) arc (0:90:1.3);
                \node[above right] at (85:0.7) {$90^\circ$};

                % --- 180° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on negative x-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.7, 0) arc (0:180:1.7);
                \node[above left] at (175:.7) {$180^\circ$};

                % --- 270° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on negative y-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.1, 0) arc (0:270:2.1);
                \node[below left] at (268:1.35) {$270^\circ$};

                % --- 360° Angle ---
                % Full rotation back to positive x-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.5, 0) arc (0:360:2.5);
                \node[below right] at (355:1.6) {$360^\circ$};

                % --- 540° Angle ---
                % 1.5 full rotations (ends on negative x-axis)
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.9, 0) arc (0:540:2.9);
                \node[above left] at (175:2.9) {$540^\circ$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            We can even talk about negative angles if we imagine they rotate clockwise (the other direction) instead.
        </p>

        <image width="60%">
            <latex-image xml:id="tikz-7-1-neg-angles">
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                % Draw Axes (without tick marks)
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-3.7, 0) -- (3.7, 0) node[right] {$x$};
                \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0, -3.7) -- (0, 3.7) node[above] {$y$};

                % ================= POSITIVE ANGLES =================
                % --- 30° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (30:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.0, 0) arc (0:30:1.0);
                \node[above right] at (5:1.0) {$30^\circ$};

                % --- 45° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (45:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.5, 0) arc (0:45:1.5);
                \node[above right] at (37:1.5) {$45^\circ$};

                % --- 60° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (60:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.0, 0) arc (0:60:2.0);
                \node[above right] at (54:2.0) {$60^\circ$};

                % --- 90° Angle ---
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.5, 0) arc (0:90:2.5);
                \node[above right] at (87:2.5) {$90^\circ$};

                % --- 180° Angle ---
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (3.0, 0) arc (0:180:3.0);
                \node[above left] at (175:3.0) {$180^\circ$};

                % ================= NEGATIVE ANGLES =================
                % --- -30° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (-30:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.0, 0) arc (0:-30:1.0);
                \node[below right] at (-5:0.9) {$-30^\circ$};

                % --- -45° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (-45:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (1.5, 0) arc (0:-45:1.5);
                \node[below right] at (-39:1.4) {$-45^\circ$};

                % --- -60° Angle ---
                \draw[thick] (0, 0) -- (-60:3.4);
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.0, 0) arc (0:-60:2.0);
                \node[below right] at (-56:1.9) {$-60^\circ$};

                % --- -90° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on negative y-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (2.5, 0) arc (0:-90:2.5);
                \node[below right] at (-88:2.5) {$-90^\circ$};

                % --- -180° Angle ---
                % Terminal side lies on negative x-axis
                \draw[-&gt;, &gt;=latex, line width=0.8pt] (3.0, 0) arc (0:-180:3.0);
                \node[below left] at (-175:3.0) {$-180^\circ$};
            \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>

        <p>
            When we draw an angle starting on the positive <m>x</m>-axis and rotate counterclockwise for a positive angle and clockwise for a negative angle, we say the angle is drawn <alert>in standard position</alert>.
        </p>
    </subsection>
	
    <subsection xml:id="ss-7-1-greek-letters">
        <title>Naming angles with Greek letters</title>
        <p>
            Textbooks often name angles using Greek letters. Two commonly used Greek letters are
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title><m>\alpha</m></title>
                    <p>
                        alpha (pronounced <q>al-fah</q>)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title><m>\beta</m></title>
                    <p>
                        beta (pronounced <q>bay-tah</q>)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            They are like the letters <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>, the first two letters of the alphabet. In fact, the word <q>alphabet</q> comes from the Greek language. So now you know.
        </p>

        <p>
            Some other common Greek letter names for angles are
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title><m>\gamma</m></title>
                    <p>
                        gamma (pronounced <q>gam-uh</q>)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title><m>\theta</m></title>
                    <p>
                        theta (pronounced <q>thay-ta</q>)
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title><m>\phi</m></title>
                    <p>
                        phi (pronounced <q>fee</q> or <q>fie</q>)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </dl>
        </p>
        
    </subsection>

